### Step 3: Generate JNI Header

Compile your Kotlin code to generate the JNI header file. You can do this by running your project and then using the `javah` tool on the generated class. This will create a header file, usually named `MainActivity.h`.

### Step 4: Implement the Native Code

Now, implement the native `applyReverbToMicrophone` function in C/C++. You can use any reverb library or implement your own algorithm. Here’s a simple example using a hypothetical reverb algorithm:

```cpp
// reverb.cpp
#include <jni.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cmath>

extern "C"
JNIEXPORT jbyteArray JNICALL
Java_com_example_yourapp_MainActivity_applyReverbToMicrophone(JNIEnv *env, jobject thiz, jbyteArray inputBuffer) {
    // Get the input buffer size
    jsize inputSize = env->GetArrayLength(inputBuffer);

    // Create output buffer
    jbyteArray outputBuffer = env->NewByteArray(inputSize);
    jbyte *inputBytes = env->GetByteArrayElements(inputBuffer, NULL);
    jbyte *outputBytes = new jbyte[inputSize];

    // Simple reverb effect (for example purposes)
    // This is a naive implementation of a reverb effect. In practice, 
    // you would use a more sophisticated algorithm.
    for (int i = 0; i < inputSize; i++) {
        // Add a simple echo (this is not true reverb)
        outputBytes[i] = inputBytes[i];
        if (i - 100 >= 0) { // Delay of 100 samples
            outputBytes[i] += inputBytes[i - 100] * 0.5; // 50% of the delayed signal
        }
    }

    // Release the input buffer
    env->ReleaseByteArrayElements(inputBuffer, inputBytes, JNI_ABORT);

    // Set the output buffer
    env->SetByteArrayRegion(outputBuffer, 0, inputSize, outputBytes);

    // Clean up
    delete[] outputBytes;

    return outputBuffer;
}
```

### Step 5: Compile the Native Code

You will need to compile the C/C++ code into a shared library (`libreverb-lib.so` on Linux/Android). You can use CMake or the NDK build system to do this.

